## EAS510 Responses - Roma
## Observed weakness in V1: what failed and why
In the baseline model, I noticed that 32 images matched and 43 were rejected, for a failing rate of 43%. While no images were falsely accepted, the model failed to specifically identify cropped photos properly - in other words, the template rule failed. The instructional guide mentioned that this template is "sensitive to rotation and scale", providing the likely reason as to why this rule failed. So, further following recommendations from the instructions, I am removing the downscale from rule 3. Therefore, both the original and the crop should stay at their real pixel size - or, ensure that they are at the same scale so the crop can line up inside the original. 
## Design decision for V2: what Rule 4 is and why you chose it
Upon execution of the revised rule 3 on the hard images in V2, I noticed that 47 photos matched their originals, and 13 did not. I went down the list of all checks and found a common error in the combination of resized and compressed images, where V1 only correctly identitfied 4 out of 10 of these pairs. While other combinations found some inconsistencies, this was the section with the largest errors. To account for this issue, I took a recommendation from the instructions and, upon doing research on the python website, I found that multi-scale template matching was the best option. 
## Effect of the change: accuracy before/after on easy vs hard
Before I made changes in V2, the model was running at 47 photos correctly matched and 13 photos that didn't match, for a correctness rate of 78.3%. With the updated model that includes rule 4 107 correctly matched and 28 were rejected, marking a very slight increase in the correctness rate to 79.3%. While this is only slightly better than the V1 code, the model ensures that there are no false rejects for the random noise photos. Taken together, rule 4 did not provide too much benefit to the model but it by no means weakened its power. 
## Trade-offs: what new costs or risks did Rule 4 introduce
First, adding rule 4 made the model much slower - it went up from about a minute to around 3-4 minutes. And, the extra time to process the results did not add much benefit in terms of increased confidence in matching the correct photos. Second, while I'm unsure how large of an issue this is, rule 4 may be duplicating some of the previous rules by singling out resizes and compressions, meaning that the same evidence may get counted twice just to reach the same result. Therefore, those overlapping rules may no longer be independent of one another. Lastly, and
